Zambia’s new political era is taking shape with the swearing-in of 40 proportional representation MPs, a historic step for inclusivity, even as the government pushes its Grow Zambia agenda on the global stage. Meanwhile, the economy shows mixed signals – a major copper smelter restart offers hope, but the real test lies in whether these developments translate into tangible benefits for ordinary Zambians.

## Main Stories

1. **Historic swearing-in of 40 PR MPs marks new era of representation** – The 40 MPs elected under the new proportional representation system – 20 women, 15 youth, and five persons with disabilities – have taken their oaths, with Speaker Nelly Mutti calling it one of the most emotional ceremonies she has presided over. This is a genuine milestone for inclusivity, but the real test will be whether these MPs wield real influence in Parliament or become symbolic voices. The process for President Hichilema’s nominated MPs is still pending, so the full composition of the House is not yet final.

2. **Zambia takes Grow Zambia agenda to UNGA81** – Foreign Affairs Minister Mulambo Haimbe is representing President Hichilema at the UN General Assembly, establishing diplomatic relations with Uruguay and Vanuatu, and engaging on financing for development. The government’s message is clear: diplomacy must help grow Zambia. But the agenda’s ambitious targets – tripling the economy, 10 million tonnes of maize, 10,000MW – require more than diplomatic goodwill; they need concrete investment commitments, and the UNGA meetings have yet to produce any announced funding or trade deals.

3. **KCM Nchanga Smelter restarts after US$40 million rehabilitation** – The smelter, with an annual capacity of 311,000 tonnes, has resumed operations after a 106-day shutdown, with Vedanta Resources touting its commitment to rebuilding KCM. This is a positive sign for copper production and jobs, but the extended shutdown – from 60 to 106 days – raises questions about the scale of deferred maintenance. The real test will be whether the smelter operates at full capacity and whether this translates into increased output and export earnings.

4. **Zambia’s government bonds gain global audience** – Zambia has qualified for J.P. Morgan’s new local-currency government bond index (GBI-EM Edge), which could attract a wider pool of investors and potentially lower borrowing costs. This is an encouraging development, but as the guest article notes, inclusion is not a formal endorsement or a guarantee of investment. The government’s strategy of enlarging bond issues to at least K10 billion per benchmark is ambitious, and the actual impact on borrowing costs will only be seen over time.

## Other Notable Stories

**Corruption and Governance:** The Anti-Corruption Commission has proposed establishing a specialised Criminal Assets Management Agency to oversee confiscated, seized, and forfeited assets. ACC Director General Daphne Chabu argues this will ensure professional management of recovered assets, but stakeholders have rightly raised concerns about duplication and institutional conflicts – the details of how this agency will interact with existing bodies remain unclear.

**Sports:** The Copper Princesses will face China in a friendly in Rabat on October 9 as part of their final preparations for the FIFA U-17 Women’s World Cup, where they are drawn with Japan, France, and Venezuela. FAZ has secured the China fixture to expose the team to Asian opposition ahead of their group-stage meeting with Japan, and is working on two more friendlies against European and South American teams. This is their third consecutive World Cup appearance – a commendable run, but the group is tough, and the team will need all the preparation it can get.

**Culture and Diplomacy:** The Chinese Embassy in Lusaka hosted a fashion show to mark the 77th anniversary of the People’s Republic of China, presented by Wonderful Group. The event underscores the growing cultural ties between Zambia and China, though it remains a soft-power exercise rather than a substantive policy development.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* **Watch the PR MPs’ legislative impact:** The 40 new PR MPs have taken their oaths, but the real question is whether they will champion substantive legislation for women, youth, and persons with disabilities, or become passive participants. Their first contributions in the House will be telling.
* **Monitor the bond index’s actual effect:** Zambia’s inclusion in the GBI-EM Edge index could lower borrowing costs, but watch for the first few bond auctions after the index launch to see if investor demand actually increases and spreads narrow.
* **Track KCM’s smelter output:** The Nchanga Smelter restart is promising, but the key metric is whether it sustains production at its 311,000-tonne annual capacity and contributes to Zambia’s three-million-tonne copper target. Any operational hiccups will be a setback for the Grow Zambia agenda.